# Help: Fault-injection framework with slab allocation failures
#
# FAILSLAB makes the slab allocator fail on demand, so the
# allocation-failure paths a healthy guest never reaches (the -ENOMEM
# returns and the unwind that follows them) get exercised. Two
# interfaces drive it. The debugfs knobs under
# /sys/kernel/debug/failslab (probability, interval, times, gated by
# FAULT_INJECTION_DEBUG_FS) fail allocations system-wide, which suits
# a soak run. The per-task counter, written to /proc/self/fail-nth,
# fails the Nth allocation of that task alone and needs no debugfs,
# which is what an in-kernel test uses to hit one call site
# deterministically.
#
# Nothing is injected until one of those knobs is set, so this
# fragment costs a guest nothing at boot.
#
# Relies on DEBUG_KERNEL and DEBUG_FS from core/core.config, as
# debug-heavy.config does. Every symbol is bool. No builtin/
# counterpart needed. The block-layer injectors (FAIL_MAKE_REQUEST,
# FAIL_IO_TIMEOUT) live with their consumer in
# storage/block-test-devices.config.
# See: lib/Kconfig.debug
# See: https://docs.kernel.org/fault-injection/fault-injection.html
CONFIG_FAULT_INJECTION=y
CONFIG_FAULT_INJECTION_DEBUG_FS=y
CONFIG_FAILSLAB=y
